course: dictionary
lesson: 9211
title: A solicitor is a lawyer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A solicitor is a kind of lawyer. Lawyers know the law, and they help people with legal problems. «A solicitor is a kind of lawyer who helps with legal things like a divorce.»
explain: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, a solicitor gives people legal advice and writes important papers, such as wills and house contracts. Clue words like «legal» and «papers» show you that the job is about the law.
explain: In the USA, the word means something different: a person who goes door to door selling things or asking for money. That is why a sign saying «No solicitors» means do not knock to sell.
explain: Seeing a solicitor does not mean you are in trouble. «Seeing one does not mean anyone is in trouble with the police.» Many ordinary families see a solicitor when they buy a house or make a will.
fact: A solicitor is a lawyer who gives people legal advice.
fact: A solicitor can write legal papers, such as wills and house contracts.
fact: «Solicitor's letters» are letters sent by a solicitor about a legal matter.
fact: In the UK, a solicitor is different from a barrister, who argues cases in the higher courts.
fact: In the USA, a solicitor can be someone who sells things door to door, so «No solicitors» means do not knock to sell.
exercise: «A solicitor is a kind of lawyer who helps with legal things like a divorce.» What is a solicitor? | A teacher who helps with school work | A police officer who catches thieves | A person who goes door to door selling things | *A kind of lawyer who gives legal advice
exercise: On Sam's street there are «solicitor's letters» about a hedge. What is a solicitor's letter? | A letter from the council about bins | *A letter from a lawyer about a legal matter | A letter asking people to buy biscuits | A birthday card from a friend
exercise: A sign on an American door says «No solicitors». What does it mean? | Please knock loudly and wait | The house is for sale | Solicitors are welcome here | *Do not knock to sell things
test: In the UK, what does a solicitor mainly do? | *Gives people legal advice and writes papers like wills | Drives the buses for the council | Cooks meals for people in court | Plays in the school football team
test: Why might a family see a solicitor when buying a house? | To go on holiday | *To get legal help with the papers | To find a lost dog | To choose new furniture
exam: Ella says «A solicitor is a kind of lawyer, so seeing one means I did something wrong.» Is Ella right? | Yes, because solicitors work for the police | No, because solicitors never give any advice | *No, getting a lawyer does not mean someone did wrong | Yes, lawyers only help people who are in trouble
